<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n8215" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">circum-jăcĕo</span></b> ,
			ĕre, v. n., <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>to lie round about</i>, <i>border upon</i> (rare; not in Cic.). </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense5"><b>(a).</b>
			<b>
				<i>Absol.</i>
			</b>, * Cael. ap. <i><span class="en">Quint. 4,
				2, 123</span></i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">populi circumjacentes,</span>”
				<i>surrounding</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">Tac. A.
					2, 72</span></i>
			. —</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense5"><b>(b).</b>
			With <i>dat.</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Lycaonia et Phrygia circumjacent Europae,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Liv. 37, 54, 11</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">capiti et collo,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">App. de Deo Socr. p. 54, 33</span></i>
			.—* </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>, in rhet.: <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">circumjăcentĭa</span></b> ,
				ĭum, n., <i>the context</i>, Qumt. 9, 4, 29.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
